# Chroma library, modern Earth: bold ways, batch 5 (bold ways stage A, 2026-10-18).
# Thirteen life events, each with a first bold way and its two links (well and badly), and second bold ways where one fits.
# Compiled with the rest of earth_play ("python3 -B build.py earth_play"); checked with check_bold.py.

# ================================================================ your closest friend dies

== bold: your closest friend dies / B
base: decide life is too short, and start taking what you want from it
likelier_if: has [striking a deal] +10; has [business partner] +5; mark 'took a wild risk' +5
- B | put your savings into the business you two always planned, and open it | v: achievement, self-direction | act: put their savings into the business the two friends always planned, and open it | chance: 40 | requires: savings | title: founder of a firm | takes_if_fails: savings
works: {N} opens the doors in the spring with the old friend's name on a small card by the till, and the firm is {Ns} own.
works: The first year is hard, but the books balance, and {N} is the founder of a firm that is still going.
fails: The money goes on stock and rent, the doors close within the year, and {Ns} savings go with them.
fails: Grief makes poor plans. By winter the firm has eaten everything {N} had put by, and it shuts.

== a partner for the friend's firm
tier: link
of_bold: your closest friend dies / B
side: well
delay: 1-3
holds: founder of a firm
stages: young_adult+
age: 19-110
stakes: .7
tone: mixed
life: work, friends
horizon: months
roles: colleague, partner
earth: the firm you opened after your friend died is busy, and someone who knew them both wants to buy in
scene: The firm {N} opened after the funeral is busier than {N} can manage alone. {colleague}, who knew the old friend too, offers to buy in, but wants the old friend's name off the card by the till.
scene gone: {colleague} has moved on, and {partner} says the firm needs a second pair of hands and fresh money, whatever name hangs over it.
- W | keep to the plan the two of you made, and turn the offer down politely | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 75
- U | go through the books with them, and see what the firm truly needs | v: self-direction | chance: 80
- B | take their money, and let the old name go with it | v: achievement, power | chance: 70
- R | tell them the name stays, and that is the end of it | v: self-direction, benevolence | chance: 75
- G | keep it small and local, the way the old friend would have liked | v: tradition, security | chance: 75
works: The firm comes through its busiest season, and {N} knows better what it is for.
works: Whatever is decided, {N} and {colleague} part on good terms.
fails: The talks drag on for months, and the work piles up meanwhile.
fails: Something said across the table about the old friend is hard to forget.
works gone: The firm comes through its busiest season, and {N} knows better what it is for.
works gone: Whatever is decided about the offer, {N} parts with it on good terms.
fails gone: The talk of money and help drags on for months, and the work piles up meanwhile.
fails gone: Something said about the old friend's name is hard to forget.

== bold: a partner for the friend's firm / U
base: go through the books with them, and see what the firm truly needs
likelier_if: has [bookkeeping] +10; has [striking a deal] +5
- U | write a fair partnership deed yourself, line by line, and sign them in | v: self-direction, achievement | act: write a fair partnership deed line by line, and sign the colleague in | chance: 40 | grants: business partner | takes_if_fails: founder of a firm
works: The deed is plain and fair, {colleague} signs it, and {N} has a business partner who pulls an equal weight.
works: Every line holds up when it is tested, and {N} and {colleague} run the firm as partners from then on.
fails: The deed has a hole in it that {N} never saw, and within a year {colleague} has the firm and {N} is out.
fails: {colleague} reads the deed better than {N} wrote it, buys up the rest, and the firm is no longer {Ns}.
works gone: The deed is plain and fair, a new partner signs it, and {N} has a business partner who pulls an equal weight.
works gone: Every line holds up when it is tested, and {N} runs the firm with a partner from then on.
fails gone: The deed has a hole in it that {N} never saw, and within a year the new partner has the firm and {N} is out.
fails gone: The new partner reads the deed better than {N} wrote it, buys up the rest, and the firm is no longer {Ns}.

